            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Snapline 2.40.0</h2>
<p>21 September 2026 · Your AI assistant can see your screen through Snapline, when you say so: a local MCP server, off until you turn it on.</p>

<h3>New</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>MCP server.</strong> Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code, Codex and Antigravity can now ask Snapline to look at a window, read an error dialog, or find a screenshot you already took, so you stop pasting screenshots into a chat. Settings → MCP. It ships off, and updating Snapline does not turn it on.</li>
  <li><strong>Three separate switches.</strong> Turning the server on lets an assistant read the captures already in your History, and nothing else. Taking new captures is its own switch. Writing copies to disk is a third, and only into one folder you choose; nothing is ever overwritten.</li>
  <li><strong>Snapline asks first.</strong> A capture raises a small Allow or Deny card under the menu bar icon that names the assistant and exactly what it wants to capture, and never takes the keyboard away from what you are typing. Choose Ask every time, Ask once per session, or Allow. No answer in 20 seconds is a no. You can also answer from the Snapline menu, with the keyboard or VoiceOver. A client's name is whatever it calls itself, and Settings says so.</li>
  <li><strong>You always know.</strong> Every capture an assistant takes shows a toast that says who took it, and the menu bar icon shows when the server is running and when a client is connected. Stopping the server, regenerating the token, or pressing Forget drops every assistant's standing approval.</li>
  <li><strong>Redactions hold.</strong> An assistant only ever receives the flattened image, so anything you redacted stays redacted; the unredacted original is never offered, at any setting. With Smart Redact after capture turned on, text read off the screen for an assistant has the same sensitive items removed and marked.</li>
  <li><strong>Text instead of pixels.</strong> An assistant can ask for the text in a window or in a capture rather than the image, laid out as plain text, a table or code. It is faster, and it costs a fraction of the tokens.</li>
  <li><strong>Scrolling Capture on request.</strong> An assistant can ask you for a Scrolling Capture of a page taller than the screen; you do the scrolling, it receives the Tall Capture. If nobody starts scrolling, Snapline puts it away.</li>
  <li><strong>Two-minute setup.</strong> Settings → MCP has a copy-ready snippet for each client with the address and token already filled in, and Install Skill adds Snapline's Agent Skill for Claude Code and Codex. The token lives in your Keychain and is copied as a concealed clipboard item, so clipboard managers keep it out of their history.</li>
  <li><strong>Local only.</strong> The server listens on 127.0.0.1 and nowhere else, refuses anything that reaches it from a web page, and runs only while Snapline is running.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
